ext} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46abd4d9-c693-5489-b647-763bf2aabe19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147029} :answer-field/id #uuid "48c22f42-8a2f-5b9a-b085-80bd413e8101" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4de3adcb-e14e-526f-9d74-b55f67fd1c9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-96558" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/9230d847daa518edfaad43f6552a48c2b3cac9523a1e9ecad8624851080e00ea.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-1$ is a source\n- $y=2$ is a sink\n- $y=2$ is a node" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-1$ points upward, and the arrow just below it points downward. In other words, both arrows point *away* from  $y=-1.$ This means that $y=-1$ is a source.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=2.$This means that $y=2$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "680a42fd-9b70-5fb2-916c-d10b71b11d82" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using Phase Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e749cb57-30c6-5395-b47b-a0225bdfcd37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318179} {:answer/id #uuid "d29d8dc6-4d9d-50d1-9af3-9b56a92cb164" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318180} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7c6b48-de2e-5567-bb55-65836844392f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318181} {:answer/id #uuid "1b6bee35-d330-5862-b8fd-88c16f973518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318182} {:answer/id #uuid "fd566b48-7f8d-5c4b-a305-4a78269892cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d29d8dc6-4d9d-50d1-9af3-9b56a92cb164" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318180} :answer-field/id #uuid "2abae91b-859d-546d-8d28-dab2e012b169" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318178}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18d0fac2-3f3b-5fc0-81b1-b5cedbfc4a46" :question/math-academy-id "q-98700" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/4c4ef140c85eb91bd01688cc2a447f13a21c2a51964c6d101887d15045cee95d.png)\n\nA particular autonomous differential equation has the phase line shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=-2$ is a sink\n- $y=-2$ is a source\n- $y=2$ is a node" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nLet's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=-2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points upward. In other words, both arrows point *toward* the point $y=-2.$This means that $y=-2$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that the arrow just above the point $y=2$ points downward, and the arrow just below it points downward too. In other words, both arrows point in the *same* direction on either side of $y=2.$ This means that $y=2$ is a node.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318198 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70100f06-56dd-5f91-9ea1-c05163b336ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318200} {:answer/id #uuid "4d67a8ab-7c36-581f-be90-fefbb9eb5c88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318201} {:answer/id #uuid "15f527b7-3019-5235-b977-583168af8f5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318202} {:answer/id #uuid "e751a523-e83d-5f62-a55c-7d20e69f7d6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318203} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd7fdcc-bc9b-5382-81eb-7cf4a201eea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15f527b7-3019-5235-b977-583168af8f5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318202} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c99b2f7-05c8-5c7e-b56c-4c32b3445515" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93b52619-a3ee-55f5-8942-7104450a0db2" :question/math-academy-id "q-98715" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/4a2c0103fb348883b05692721162ffba17758beb0e6f944deb328a506fed8d21.png)\n\nConsiderer the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=4$ is a source.\n- $y=2$ is a node.\n- $y=-1$ is a sink." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/df340c40982e6d22ef8a815bf043f84fd78b43b4ddc1763845d03d4b2c793aee.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=4$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=2$ is a source.\n\n- Statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a sink.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318205 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4535b48e-a33f-5f62-95d3-a5c0991c1f6d" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibrium Points Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b70ad7f-1fe9-5b11-a0d5-ace26bf2aa94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318207} {:answer/id #uuid "78e38bb7-38ae-5c70-8810-997bf249da59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318208} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b6c43b-d176-5602-9953-4c6b81f061d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318209} {:answer/id #uuid "2836ffe3-3d50-5f28-854e-f1df42512859" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318210} {:answer/id #uuid "8908aead-f581-59e9-a80d-2d52eb89778a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5b6c43b-d176-5602-9953-4c6b81f061d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318209} :answer-field/id #uuid "04cce166-b33d-540b-92e7-c67589729173" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ece7eaa-a6b2-5c98-ac8f-17a221444654" :question/math-academy-id "q-98752" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/3114d1c94b63a2506a66e701346bc7367b901659b78a2cf7c191648c96a716dc.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y)$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y=3$ is a node.\n- $y=-1$ is a sink.\n- $y=-1$ is a source." :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nThe phase line that corresponds to the given graph is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/f72f89ecad74cc708b5950763002757d9d012d3859c7a5bd5eb19ae60fc54e55.png)\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n- Statement I is false. From the phase line, we see that $y=3$ is a sink.\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. From the phase line, we see that $y=-1$ is a source.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying the Equilibrium Points of a Given Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55758d1e-d33b-5677-8be9-3f33f97c7154" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a source, and $y=3$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318228} {:answer/id #uuid "7a9a6079-f384-506c-8a9c-c8afe0517518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a node, and $y=3$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318229} {:answer/id #uuid "42b4755d-8763-5111-ac50-ca89e32e4d8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318230} {:answer/id #uuid "7d606246-48cf-5740-bc21-e71d80e0e300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a node, and $y=3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318231} {:answer/id #uuid "270a9c82-ba27-54ff-8b05-bf68acf4d9e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a sink, and $y=3$ is a node" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a9a6079-f384-506c-8a9c-c8afe0517518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a node, and $y=3$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318229} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4ec143c-b984-58eb-93ce-e72cae8bb019" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1da3364-5c3f-500b-bc7c-7a5f92c432cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-98760" :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=-(y-3)({y}^{2}+6y+9)?$" :question/worked-solution "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ -(y-3)({y}^{2}+6y+9) & =0 \\\\ -(y-3)(y+3)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y & =-3,3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-3$ and $y=3.$\n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-(y-3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| $(y+3)^{2}$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,3),$ and negative for $y\\in (3,\\infty ).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042839/assets/3c118c900486d8561864c88e1b25a461c8f459f810bb7b753d30d9d0d4780913.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solutions:\n\n- $y=-3$  is a node because it is neither a source nor a sink.\n\n- $y=3$ is a sink because the nearby solutions converge toward this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=-3$ is a node, and $y=3$ is a sink\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b114a373-697f-5005-bb1b-46202547a905" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying the Equilibrium Points of a Given Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bcf6f5e-187a-5faf-9b08-2811fdecda6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a node" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318235} {:answer/id #uuid "c6838c90-d12a-5863-96d3-0456aebfc28c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a sink" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318236} {:answer/id #uuid "01fe33c8-1b70-519a-a364-f3103a3bd5ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y=-3$ is a source" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318237} {:answer/id #uuid "06f21726-8030-5fc9-a7a9-